From: Seemless contributors
Subject: [PATCH] ui/seemless: export scanouts as IOSurfaces over Mach ports

Why: the Seemless app draws the guest itself (docs/04, decision D4).
QEMU's -display dbus cannot drive virtio-gpu-gl on macOS (no EGL init, no
macOS texture export, D-Bus cannot carry Mach ports; spike S3), so this
adds a small display backend instead. One new file in ui/, a QAPI
enum/struct and one meson line; nothing in virtio-gpu or Neptune code.
Applies to utmapp/qemu 8ab298bc52158e52bbaf91b7f91b75d46d7384c9.

Usage
  -display seemless,gl=es,service=NAME
  gl=es is required for virtio-gpu-gl (ANGLE/Metal, as -display cocoa).
  SEEMLESS_REFRESH_MS (environment) overrides the 30 ms refresh timer that
  paces 2D (ramfb, firmware, Basic Display) updates.

Protocol, version 1
  Transport: Mach messages. QEMU checks in a receive port with
  bootstrap_check_in(NAME) at start-up and exits if the name is taken.
  Every message QEMU receives must carry a security trailer whose uid is
  QEMU's own uid; anything else, malformed or unknown is destroyed.
  Integers are host-endian (little), structs naturally aligned, fields
  follow the mach_msg_header_t (24 bytes) in this order.

  Client -> QEMU (to NAME)
    0x53450001 CONNECT   complex: 1 port descriptor = send right to the
                         client's receive port; u32 version (=1), u32 flags (0).
                         Up to 8 clients. QEMU answers with SURFACES (each
                         scanout that has a ring), CURSOR, MOUSE, then a fresh
                         FRAME.
    0x53450002 ACK       u32 head, generation, index, pad. "I am reading this
                         slot": QEMU will not overwrite it while it has any
                         other slot free. msgh_local_port = the client's port
                         (MAKE_SEND), which identifies the client.
    0x53450003 SET_SIZE  u32 head, width, height, width_mm, height_mm, pad.
                         Forwarded as dpy_set_ui_info (virtio-gpu: display
                         info / EDID); the guest driver decides whether to
                         switch. Accepted range 320x200 .. 16384x16384.

  QEMU -> client (to the client's port; never blocks: send timeout 0, a
  full queue drops the message, a dead port drops the client)
    0x53451001 SURFACES  complex: 3 port descriptors (MOVE_SEND, from
                         IOSurfaceCreateMachPort) = the scanout's ring;
                         u32 version, head, generation, width, height,
                         fourcc ('BGRA' or 'RGBA', 4 bytes per pixel, alpha
                         undefined). Sent again, with generation+1, whenever
                         size or format changes. Clients must check each
                         surface's size and format against the header.
    0x53451002 FRAME     u32 head, generation, index, source (1 = CPU copy of
                         a 2D surface, 2 = GPU blit of a virgl Metal scanout);
                         u64 serial; u32 x, y, w, h (currently the whole
                         scanout); u64 t_update, t_ready (mach_absolute_time
                         when the frame reached QEMU's display code and when
                         its pixels were in the slot). The slot stays intact
                         until at least the next two frames, or while ACKed.
    0x53451003 DISABLE   u32 head, pad. The GL scanout went away; 2D frames
                         (if any) follow.
    0x53451004 CURSOR    complex: 1 OOL descriptor = width*height u32 pixels
                         0xAARRGGBB; u32 head, serial, width, height;
                         i32 hot_x, hot_y. At most 256x256.
    0x53451005 MOUSE     u32 head; i32 x, y; u32 visible. Guest pointer
                         position (dpy_mouse_set).

  Heads are QEMU's graphic consoles in index order (at most 4).
  Input is not part of this protocol: clients use QMP input-send-event.

How frames are made
  2D surfaces: full CPU copy into the next free slot on every update
  (slots lag, so damage alone is not enough). Metal scanouts: one GPU blit
  at RESOURCE_FLUSH (dpy_gl_update), one in flight, because the guest's
  buffer is only guaranteed intact from the flush to the next flip (same
  rule as ui/cocoa.m). Nothing is copied while no client is connected.
  GL-only scanouts (no native Metal texture) are not exported.

